# Critique review — local biomedical image review workspace

**Review basis.** I read the supplied brief (`inputs/brief.md`, SHA-256 `27311c714607087ce93df4544c37fa3adf871a12ec77e4f9e8896565e0a4e628`), the complete frozen proposal (`inputs/prior/research/research/proposal.md`, `20ca045c1d1b3900bcc4b15eec9c8ca78d0004b4dcb4f99cfeac99871a6274a3`), and its source, witness, leads, and NGFF line-map artifacts (SHA-256 respectively `fe46e1c59a72e5a645ce3c1356ff48ac115e5293b043e319f96de0304165cd5a`, `edd75e6e07f5cce3abc5fc37a45b4b5b883f279b5e969956cc9d2505909ec589`, `ebc91799a1b4a86abd27560da086d01742215eaa9ae5544e74bf51835c4a96f6`, and `7a68bf2d4aa7881eb6623bcbdbd0092ef4714562fdcfd76377ab256ef6305274`). I independently checked the consequential points below against the pinned public primary versions. No application build, package install, performance run, or application test was performed for this review.

## Assessment

The proposal is a coherent incremental plan, and its main architecture remains a reasonable hypothesis: keep originals read-only; use a viewer and a focused TIFF reader as separate components; keep the editable project model authoritative; use chunked data only for suitable derivatives, masks, or exchange; and qualify the real package combination and machine before claiming performance or interoperability. The sources support mechanisms, not a complete application or its 3 GB target. The proposal is unusually clear about that limit and about deferred ideas. I found two substantive contract gaps and two precision improvements; these call for targeted revision, not wholesale rejection.

## Findings that affect the plan

### C1 — The initial axes allowlist omits images with both time and channel axes

**External fact.** OME-NGFF 0.4 §3.4 describes 2–5D image arrays with 2 or 3 spatial axes, optionally one time axis, and optionally one channel axis; §3.4 gives an ordering in which time precedes channel and spatial axes. Its §3.1 specifies axis names/types/units and their correspondence to array dimensions. The inspected source is the [OME-NGFF 0.4 report](https://ngff.openmicroscopy.org/0.4/), §3.1 and §3.4 (capture `7fedbaf1ca3645cab28a245286d92043`, SHA-256 `ca4780a33f9561cfd2d983651fdcc745dce0d6089f5cf775744a54e8394a5269`).

**Correction and product choice.** Proposal §2 lists `YX`, `ZYX`, `CYX`, `CZYX`, `TYX`, and `TZYX`; none combines `T` and `C`, although the surrounding prose calls both optional and the brief explicitly allows both on an image. Add at least representative `TCYX` and `TCZYX` fixtures to the intended first support boundary, with any reader axis permutation mapped explicitly. If the first milestone deliberately excludes combined T+C inputs, name that as a staged deferral and ensure the initial workflow still supports channel and time selection on the relevant accepted acquisitions. The project/display/annotation/export contract must keep T and C selectors distinct from spatial coordinates.

**Affected dependencies.** Support-boundary statement in §2, axis normalization, saved display state, annotation selectors, and fixture/round-trip checks P1, P2, and P5. This is a scope omission in the proposal, not an NGFF incompatibility.

### C2 — Masks need the same explicit source and geometry linkage as vector annotations

**Plan obligation.** The proposal gives points and polygons an image hash, geometry revision, named coordinates, scope, selectors, and provenance. It then says only that masks live in a chunked label array with label values and class names. That does not yet say how an editable mask is tied to its source asset and geometry revision, what its named axes mean, or whether it is slice/time-local, volumetric, or acquisition-wide. The brief requires the relationship to remain inspectable after transformation and export.

**Correction and product choice.** Specify a mask record alongside the array: source content identity and geometry revision; mask array axes/order, shape and index-to-world mapping; explicit spatial/time/channel scope or selectors; label IDs and names; edit revision/provenance; and, for a derived image or mask, the recorded mapping to its input. The record can point to a NGFF label group for raster interchange, but the project remains authoritative for the editable mask and its scope. The proposal correctly warns that NGFF labels do not define the entire project model; keep that distinction.

**Proposed validation.** P2/P5 should include a fixed-plane mask and a volume mask, with nonzero origin and anisotropic spacing, then round-trip each through save/reopen and raster export. Check that a crop/resample creates a new mapped mask or explicit derivative and never silently retargets the original mask. **UNEXECUTED.**

### C3 — Keep SQLite rollback-journal and WAL recovery claims distinct

**External fact.** SQLite’s [Atomic Commit](https://sqlite.org/atomiccommit.html) article explicitly describes rollback-journal mode and says WAL uses a different commit mechanism (capture `4bba3369c0814421be84649bc10b84d6`, SHA-256 `5a5be7b660217c9408c8a81eaf2faa2d08832ed3790f895a0a713916ad856ea1`). The [WAL documentation](https://sqlite.org/wal.html), last modified 2026-09-11, says WAL requires processes to share memory on the same host and does not work over a network filesystem; it also says the `-wal` file may contain committed transactions and is part of persistent database state until safely checkpointed (capture `4ece0f07ba344ed59a817a214035bf6e`, SHA-256 `f3467b530b883d4a00574fe1a898b3d121ed72764ae28cf66941080ac0badb9e`).

**Preserved claim and precision correction.** The proposal is right to keep the editable database local, exchange closed snapshots, qualify the actual shared storage, and not assume SQLite durability on arbitrary filesystems. When describing a snapshot, distinguish `-wal` transaction content from `-shm`, which is the WAL shared-memory index; the docs do not give them identical persistence semantics. Keeping the complete live set together until a tested close/checkpoint is a safe operational rule, but do not present both sidecars as equivalent durable database contents. State one concrete local journal/backup policy: either use the default rollback-journal path with its tested recovery sequence, or use WAL only on the local workstation and make the SQLite backup/clean-close/checkpoint step part of snapshot publication. A database transaction also cannot atomically commit a separate asset copy or archive; the staged `.partial` asset plus verified manifest/generation pattern in §4 is therefore important and should cover every derived asset/export publication.

**Affected dependencies.** §4 save, copy, and exchange sequence; L6 shared-storage recovery; P4. The sources do not qualify the group’s particular filesystem or the proposal’s recovery implementation.

### C4 — The napari issue-to-fix-to-test chain is real; preserve its exact scope

**External fact and chain.** napari issue [#9247](https://api.github.com/repos/napari/napari/issues/9247) describes a reproducible bug in which selected or hovered Shapes outlines could remain visible on an adjacent slice while the shape face was hidden; it reports 0.8.x and a `main` merge-base environment. The fresh issue capture is `915c1de052f44d159d8a7e3cff9b8e66`, SHA-256 `6b5651cf32b79e433a8cb4cb41517d549e539b1166eb09a9875aa6410f490550`.

The full [#9059 patch](https://github.com/napari/napari/pull/9059.patch) changes Shapes view filtering and adds `test_outline_not_drawn_off_slice`, which exercises selected and hover-only outlines on and off the slice. Its broader tests also preserve logical selection across slice/display changes and check that the interaction box follows the in-view shapes. The captured patch is `f901ac50d2264eaa8b27df2de6055be3`, SHA-256 `70c80afd76610be6010e341191d33342ec976be557738d9fdd9feffc56512cce`; the merge commit recorded in the frozen source card is `de87bdd0be61a3f63e643865553277ab22a04ac8` (2026-08-12).

The [#9399 patch](https://github.com/napari/napari/pull/9399.patch) makes the `_indices_view` → `_view_indices` rename in both implementation and test. It is an integration/name correction, not another behavioral regression test. Its patch capture is `5cb80c3c67224d86b6dbbd36a3ac65e9`, SHA-256 `7553dba1659964fd204a94c79aea972c8daad0162cda92acb7fb1f47f0d5b216`; patch commit `dabd9b8fdce862581d2c4bfcd939b926f014173e`. The frozen PR-body capture S12 (`b6ef55376b8b4acd935e40e371000c21`, SHA-256 `93444588641a78ea44a6c9e5c5234926204e92115e27c7c9ab5d8336cff8cc3d`) records the maintainer’s explanation that the earlier merge was incomplete and broke code. A fresh REST request for that PR was rate-limited (HTTP 403), so that explanation relies on the complete frozen primary-source capture; the newly fetched patch independently confirms the exact code/test rename.

Release applicability is strengthened by inspecting the exact v0.9.0 tag target `908b3ed80ccbe73a3393f0207aafc6d7a45e116a`: the pinned [Shapes test file](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/napari/napari/908b3ed80ccbe73a3393f0207aafc6d7a45e116a/src/napari/layers/shapes/_tests/test_shapes.py) contains the off-slice regression test (capture `482b8e7d115c4dd7bf8fabfb518b4a5d`, SHA-256 `0ce384062ceb2f9d9096d1dd445747938641dbfbca2eb64030bb1976fb986fe5`), and the pinned [implementation](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/napari/napari/908b3ed80ccbe73a3393f0207aafc6d7a45e116a/src/napari/layers/shapes/shapes.py) contains in-view filtering and the follow-up name (capture `1141eef95cba4945acebb498e876647d`, SHA-256 `4e7ac1b7b1c4b3163b34b6e2688e81745beea3a9043629594046894d57247a30`). The frozen compare capture S14 was truncated, so do not claim a full release ancestry audit from it; the exact release source does establish that the test and corrected implementation are present at the tag. None of these artifacts proves that the tests ran in the proposed packaged application.

**Preserved lesson and dependency.** The proposal correctly separates view-dependent highlight rendering from annotation-coordinate persistence and proposes an exact-pin regression check. Make the UI contract explicit: an off-slice shape can remain logically selected while its outline/handles are not drawn on the current slice. Keep selected and hover-only cases in the napari 0.9.0 regression set, and recheck on any upgrade. This precedent says nothing about napari project save/reopen, image calibration, or export fidelity; those remain app-owned tests. The mentioned `_view_indices`/other underscore-prefixed names are implementation details, not an application API to depend on.

### C5 — Zarr claims hold; cite the exact documentation version

**External fact.** The versioned [Zarr-Python v3.4.0 arrays guide](https://zarr.readthedocs.io/en/v3.4.0/user-guide/arrays/) says slicing loads the requested region into a NumPy array; a whole-array slice therefore materializes the whole selection. The versioned [v3.4.0 migration guide](https://zarr.readthedocs.io/en/v3.4.0/user-guide/v3_migration/) says newly created arrays default to Zarr format 3, `zarr_format=2` selects format 2, and the v3 API includes breaking changes. Captures: arrays `d77f1b4cdaa34f89b6b009fbd4643d22`, SHA-256 `9dd614d41f086f795047344af03bb7cde3bead96e82638c5392ed7f9469a1e89`; migration `3111500ab9514bf4aae2784798d03f22`, SHA-256 `674b34bdebfc30854e9073dfd55dea683c8af2afa8abbed6f81dddbc51411d6c`.

**Correction.** The predecessor catalog cites stable URLs and infers the release context. Replace those locators with the versioned v3.4.0 URLs above, so the documentation pin follows the implementation pin. The proposal’s explicit format-2 choice for NGFF 0.4 is supported. Neither these docs nor the TIFF reader docs establish that the integrated viewer, decoder, cache, and display pipeline meet the memory/latency goal.

### C6 — Treat full-file hashing as an I/O and recovery operation

**Engineering inference.** A streaming SHA-256 avoids a second full in-memory copy, but it still reads the complete 3 GB file. The [tifffile v2026.9.20 release notes](https://github.com/cgohlke/tifffile/releases/tag/v2026.9.20) describe NumPy/Zarr-array/group access across TIFF strips, tiles, pages, SubIFDs, series, and pyramid levels (capture `f64f72618bbc48f0886fff9d069d8a4a`, SHA-256 `7172fb4e4c8b4b5c7298ca9977c92466377956d6923c4a8dc984eefb38347904`). This confirms useful reader mechanisms, not access granularity, codec coverage, or bounded integrated memory. The predecessor already makes that limitation clear.

**Proposed validation.** Extend P1 to measure the initial identity pass and relink/hash path, not only navigation: bytes read, peak RSS including decoder/in-flight buffers and display copies, progress, cancellation latency, and behavior while identity is provisional. Confirm the file did not change during the pass before finalizing annotations/exports against its hash. Retain the proposed per-layout/codec tests and do not claim the 3 GB target until the exact reader-viewer stack passes. **UNEXECUTED.**

## Supported claims and ideas to preserve

- The independent precedents contribute distinct mechanisms: napari is a multidimensional interactive viewer and Shapes editing layer; tifffile is a TIFF and TIFF-like reader with array/Zarr access; Zarr/OME-NGFF supplies chunked storage and a multidimensional metadata/interchange convention. These are independent projects/standards, not evidence that the integrated stack works. The checked pins are napari v0.9.0 at the tag target above, tifffile v2026.9.20, Zarr-Python v3.4.0, and OME-NGFF 0.4.
- The proposed NGFF coordinate lesson is correct: §3.3 lists identity, scale, and translation transformations and says they are applied sequentially in order; it does not define the proposed general affine with arbitrary rotation/shear. Keep the full project geometry and either export an explicit transform sidecar/index-space representation or refuse a physical-space standard export that would lose it. The executed W1 arithmetic check only checks candidate-authored scale-then-translation arithmetic; it does not test an NGFF parser/writer or an application.
- The narrow TIFF boundary, explicit handling of unknown axes/calibration, preservation of raw metadata, immutable sources, content-based relinking, distinct derived-operation records, and closed verified bundle exchange all respond to the brief. The source and actual instrument fixture corpus remain critical prerequisites. The Bio-Formats v8.4.0 selective-reader option remains a plausible bounded alternative, not proof of universal support.
- Keep the NGFF multiscale/label opportunity deferred behind independent-reader and workflow checks; keep TIFF as canonical with selective NGFF derivatives as the plausible alternative. Closed bundle exchange remains aligned with the six-person workflow. Live collaboration, cloud services, segmentation, and registration are correctly separated as later options.
- Keep calibration pixel-center convention, arbitrary-affine export, exact decoder granularity, package interoperability, filesystem publication semantics, and schema migration/compatibility across workstations as unresolved until tested. The proposal’s P1–P6 checks are correctly marked proposed/UNEXECUTED; no candidate execution receipt or full-application behavior should be inferred from them.

## Review disposition

Revise the accepted-axis examples to cover combined T+C or explicitly defer that class, complete the mask-to-source/geometry/scope record, and make the SQLite journal/snapshot language precise. Strengthen the Zarr source URLs to versioned documentation and include whole-file identity time in the 3 GB qualification. Keep the rest of the architecture and deferred alternatives pending the proposal’s own fixture-based, target-machine validation. This review is a source-backed critique, not evidence that an implementation or any unexecuted validation has passed.
